---
# Layout prompt for the panel.
#
# This dotprompt asset is the layout: the grid the components are placed on,
# the proportion each card is built for, and the rules every screen follows.
# It belongs to the demo's wall panel, not to the catalog, so it lives with the
# other backend assets and is rendered at startup between the A2UI mechanics
# prompt and the catalog prompt.
#
# The proportions are the ones on the Figma component catalog and the screens
# built from it. They are written once, in the catalog's `GenUiComponent.fit`,
# and this file only says them: the backend checks every reply against the same
# sizes (`LayoutValidator`) and the frontend draws exactly them. Change a size in
# the catalog, never here.
#
# Handlebars variables:
#   cardFits - one line per card with the proportions it is built for, rendered
#              from the components' own `fit`
---

## The grid

The card area is a grid of 12 columns and 2 rows. Think in proportions of it,
never in sizes: 4 of 12 columns is a third of the width, 6 is a half, 8 is two
thirds and 12 is all of it. A card is half tall (one row) or full height (both
rows). The `ActionChips` row sits below the area and is not part of the grid.

A card's width is its `weight`: the number of the 12 columns it spans. Every
card is built for particular proportions and for no others. Never give a card a
`weight` it does not list, and never stretch a card to fill a gap: pick other
cards, or other spans of the cards you have, that fill the row.

{{{cardFits}}}

## Putting a screen together

A screen is a root `Column` that holds the cards and ends with the
`ActionChips`. The cards are laid out in one of two ways.

**Two rows.** The root holds two `Row`s, one for each row of the grid. Each `Row`
holds one to three cards, and the `weight`s in it add up to exactly 12.

```
root (Column)  children: ["row1", "row2", "chips"]
row1 (Row)     children: ["trip", "charge"]       weights 7, 5
row2 (Row)     children: ["a", "b", "c"]          weights 4, 4, 4
chips          ActionChips
```

**A full height card.** When the screen has a full height card (a `PlanTimeline`
with `density: detailed`, a `ShoppingList`, or an urgent `HeadsUpCard`), the
root holds a single `Row` with the whole area. It has two children: the full
height card, with its `weight`, and one `Column` beside it. That `Column` has the
`weight` of the columns the card leaves (12 less its own), and holds one or two
`Row`s of half tall cards. The `weight`s in each of those `Row`s add up to the
`Column`'s `weight`, in the same way the `weight`s of a `Row` of the whole grid
add up to 12.

```
root (Column)  children: ["body", "chips"]
body (Row)     children: ["list", "rest"]
list           ShoppingList, weight 5
rest (Column)  weight 7   children: ["rest1", "rest2"]
rest1 (Row)    children: ["oven", "fridge"]       weights 4, 3
rest2 (Row)    children: ["lights", "scene"]      weights 3, 4
chips          ActionChips
```

Spans that add up to 12 with the cards of the catalog: 7 and 5, 8 and 4, 4 and
8, 5 and 7, three of 4, or a 3, a 4 and a 5 in any order. Beside a full height
card the rows add up to what it leaves: 7 is a 7, or a 4 and a 3; 8 is an 8, or
two of 4, or a 3 and a 5; 4 is a single card of 4. A `DeviceCard` is the card
that fits most sums, because it is built for 3, 4 or 5. A row of two cards
never adds up to 12 unless one of them is a 7 or an 8, so two cards of 4 or 5
need a third.

Rules for every screen:

- End the root with exactly one `ActionChips`, below the cards, with 1 to 3
  suggested next steps, unless the screen has an urgent `HeadsUpCard`, which
  has none. It is the root's last child; defining it without listing it in
  `root.children` means it is never shown. Do not forget it.
- Fill the grid. Every row adds up to its columns, with no gap and no card
  stretched. A screen is usually four or five cards, at most six, counting the
  `HeadsUpCard` and the `ChangeSummary`; the `ActionChips` row is not one. Send
  the cards that matter to this person, most important first. If they leave
  the grid short, the panel adds devices to fill it after your cards, so do not
  add a device only to fill space, and do not leave one of your cards out to
  make the sums work.
- The `HeadsUpCard` and the `ChangeSummary` lead: they go in the top row. At most
  one of each.
- An urgent `HeadsUpCard` (`variant: urgent`) is the full height card of its
  screen, 8 columns wide, with a `Column` of 4 beside it.
- A full height card is the only one on the screen: never two. When the screen
  wants both the `ShoppingList` and a `PlanTimeline`, the list is the full
  height card and the plan is `density: compact`, in a half tall cell beside
  it.
- Set a `weight` on every card in a `Row`, and on the `Column` beside the full
  height card. A card with none is drawn at the span it is best built for.
- Rank the cards first, then place them: the higher a card ranks, the bigger
  the cell it gets, among the cells it is built for. When the ranked cards do
  not fit the grid, the lowest ranked are left out.
- Every id a `Column` or `Row` lists must be defined, and every component you
  define must be listed by exactly one parent.
- Give every component a stable id and reuse it when you re-send it.
